---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const installCommand =
  "curl -fsSL https://ok200.app/install-crostini.sh | bash";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install 200 OK Linux on ChromeOS"
  description="Install and control the lightweight 200 OK Linux server on x86_64 or ARM64 Chromebooks and compatible x86_64 ChromeOS Flex devices."
>
  <Header />

  <main class="guide-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">ChromeOS Linux</p>
        <h1>Use 200 OK without Android or Google Play</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          This is the full-control ChromeOS option: install one small signed
          Linux component, then configure, start, stop, and update it from the
          Chrome extension—without Google Play.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container narrow content">
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Before you start:</strong> your Chromebook and profile must
        allow the Linux development environment. Older devices, child
        accounts, and managed work or school profiles may not provide it.
        ChromeOS Flex support also varies by model and is x86_64 only.
      </div>

      <h2>Install</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open ChromeOS Settings → About ChromeOS → Developers → Linux
          development environment, then choose <strong>Set up</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>Open Terminal after Linux finishes installing.</li>
        <li>
          Paste this command and press Enter:
          <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          Open <strong>200 OK Linux</strong> from the ChromeOS Launcher. The
          extension will ask once for permission to communicate with the local
          Linux controller.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The installer detects x86_64 or ARM64, verifies the signed release
        manifest, and installs only for your Linux user. It does not use sudo,
        enable an always-on service, or start serving a folder.
      </p>

      <h2>After a reboot</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>200 OK Linux</strong> from the ChromeOS Launcher whenever
        you want to use it. That Launcher item wakes Linux and opens the
        extension controller. If ChromeOS cannot wake it, open Terminal once,
        wait for the prompt, close Terminal, and try the Launcher item again.
      </p>

      <h2>Choose a folder</h2>
      <p>
        The default is <code>~/Downloads/200 OK</code> under Linux files. To
        serve a Chromebook folder, right-click it in the Files app, choose
        <strong>Share with Linux</strong>, then enter its
        <code>/mnt/chromeos/…</code> path in the controller. Linux cannot read
        unshared ChromeOS folders.
      </p>

      <h2>Reach it from another device</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Start the server in the extension controller.</li>
        <li>
          Open ChromeOS Settings → About ChromeOS → Developers → Linux
          development environment → Port forwarding.
        </li>
        <li>
          Add the content port shown by 200 OK (8080 by default) as TCP and
          turn it on.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use the Chromebook&apos;s Wi-Fi IPv4 address, shown in its network
          details, from the other device.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout warning">
        Forward only the content port you selected. Never forward controller
        port 20080.
      </div>

      <h2>Updates and removal</h2>
      <p>
        The controller checks the signed update channel while Linux is already
        running. Automatic installation is opt-in and waits until the web
        server is stopped. These recovery commands are also available in
        Terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>ok200-crostini check-update
ok200-crostini update
ok200-crostini rollback
ok200-crostini uninstall</code></pre>
      <p>
        Normal uninstall preserves controller settings for a later reinstall.
        <code>ok200-crostini uninstall --purge</code> also removes pairing and
        update state. Neither command deletes served folders.
      </p>

      <p class="back"><a href="/chromeos">← Compare ChromeOS options</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .guide-page {
    min-height: 70vh;
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .hero {
    padding: 72px 0 32px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 8px 0 16px;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: 680px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .content {
    padding-bottom: 80px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .content h2 {
    margin: 36px 0 10px;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .content li + li {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }

  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 14px 0;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .callout {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
  }

  .callout.warning {
    border-left-color: #d97706;
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 42px;
  }

  .back a {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .hero {
      padding-top: 48px;
    }

    .content ol {
      padding-left: 22px;
    }
  }
</style>
